NAME
       nmap - network exploration tool and security / port scanner

SYNOPSIS
       nmap [-sT | -sS] [-sV] [-sn] [-Pn] [-p PORTS] [--open] TARGET...

DESCRIPTION
       nmap finds which hosts are up and which network ports they answer on.
       For each port it reports a state:

       open      a program accepts connections on the port.
       closed    the host answered, but nothing listens on the port.
       filtered  a firewall blocked the probe, so nmap cannot tell.

       Scan only systems you own or have written permission to test: a port
       scan of someone else's network can break the law and their policies.
       On the practice servers nmap scans the simulated lab network only.

       By default nmap scans the 1000 most common TCP ports and lists the ones
       that are not in the most common state ("Not shown: ..."). nmap is in
       the nmap package (dnf install nmap).

OPTIONS
       -sT    A TCP connect scan (completes the handshake; any user may run it).

       -sS    A SYN ("half-open") scan; needs root. On the practice servers it
              gives the same results as -sT.

       -sV    Probe open ports to find the service and its version.

       -sn    Host discovery only: is the host up? No port scan.

       -Pn    Treat the host as up (skip discovery).

       -p PORTS
              Scan these ports: 22, 22,80,443 or a range 1-1024.

       --open Show only open ports.

EXAMPLES
       Which common ports answer on server2?

           nmap server2

       Which hosts answer on a whole network (a TARGET may be a network
       written ADDRESS/BITS, up to /22)?

           nmap -sn 10.10.1.0/24

       Is SSH, HTTP or HTTPS open, and what software is behind it?

           nmap -sV -p 22,80,443 server2

SEE ALSO
       ss(8), firewall-cmd(1), curl(1)
